    "turns": [
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Why does ReLU deserve its own place in the history of artificial intelligence?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "In 1969, Kunihiko Fukushima used a rectifying response in an NHK multilayer neural-network study, but the idea was not widely adopted at the time. Systematic experiments in 2010–2011 re-established the training advantages of rectified units and helped make them a standard activation choice.",
            "sourceIds": [
                "source-fukushima-analog-threshold-elements",
                "source-rectified-linear-units-improve-restricted-boltzmann-machines",
                "source-deep-sparse-rectifier-neural-networks"
            ],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Negative responses are clipped to zero while positive responses pass through linearly.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-deep-sparse-rectifier-neural-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat ReLU as a simple engineering choice whose impact was larger than its formula suggests. Its long-term legacy is the non-saturating activation pattern that helped deep networks train reliably and became a default component of modern architectures.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-deep-sparse-rectifier-neural-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "As today's systems grow larger, which lessons from ReLU still matter, and which limits deserve to be questioned again?",
            "sourceIds": [
                "source-fukushima-analog-threshold-elements",
                "source-deep-sparse-rectifier-neural-networks",
                "source-rectified-linear-units-improve-restricted-boltzmann-machines",
